1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,570
You think you're sitting still.

2
00:00:03,497 --> 00:00:05,703
You're not. Not even close.

3
00:00:06,916 --> 00:00:13,071
Right now, without feeling a thing, you're
moving at well over a million kilometres an hour.

4
00:00:14,284 --> 00:00:15,330
Let's add it up.

5
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:21,712
One level at a time. And let's keep a running
count of how far you travel while you watch.

6
00:00:26,212 --> 00:00:28,260
First, the ground beneath you.

7
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:37,430
Earth turns once a day, and you turn with it, sweeping around a
circle that, at the equator, is forty thousand kilometres long.

8
00:00:38,357 --> 00:00:44,515
At the equator, that's about one thousand,
six hundred and seventy kilometres an hour.

9
00:00:45,727 --> 00:00:50,564
Away from the equator, your circle
is smaller, so you move more slowly.

10
00:00:50,564 --> 00:00:55,832
In London, about one thousand and
forty. At the poles, almost nothing.

11
00:01:00,332 --> 00:01:02,249
But Earth itself is moving.

12
00:01:02,249 --> 00:01:07,931
It circles the Sun once a year, at
nearly thirty kilometres every second.

13
00:01:08,858 --> 00:01:12,706
That's about a hundred and seven
thousand kilometres an hour.

14
00:01:12,706 --> 00:01:15,856
London to New York in about three minutes.

15
00:01:20,356 --> 00:01:23,214
And the Sun is not standing still either.

16
00:01:23,214 --> 00:01:30,335
It carries every planet with it, so our real path
is not a circle. It's a long, slanted spiral.

17
00:01:31,262 --> 00:01:35,956
The planets' orbits are tilted about
sixty degrees to the Sun's path.

18
00:01:35,956 --> 00:01:50,880
Not the swirling vortex you may have seen online. The real spiral is far gentler. Each year, the Sun moves
about fifty times farther than the width of Earth's orbit, so our path is stretched out, almost straight.

19
00:01:55,380 --> 00:02:00,160
Because the Sun is orbiting too,
around the centre of the Milky Way.

20
00:02:01,087 --> 00:02:04,618
At roughly two hundred and
thirty kilometres a second.

21
00:02:04,618 --> 00:02:08,191
Over eight hundred thousand
kilometres an hour.

22
00:02:09,404 --> 00:02:13,990
Even so, one lap takes around two
hundred and thirty million years.

23
00:02:13,990 --> 00:02:21,266
The last time the Sun was at this point in its
orbit, the first dinosaurs were only just appearing.

24
00:02:22,478 --> 00:02:31,036
And as it circles, it drifts gently up and down through the
galaxy's disk, crossing it every thirty million years or so.

25
00:02:35,536 --> 00:02:40,879
Then the Milky Way itself is moving,
and so is every galaxy around it.

26
00:02:41,806 --> 00:02:46,107
All of space is filled with a
faint afterglow of the Big Bang:

27
00:02:46,107 --> 00:02:55,418
the cosmic microwave background. Measured against it, the Sun is
moving at about three hundred and seventy kilometres a second.

28
00:02:56,345 --> 00:02:58,542
These speeds don't simply add up.

29
00:02:58,542 --> 00:03:06,498
They point in different directions. But the total is still
more than one point three million kilometres an hour.

30
00:03:07,711 --> 00:03:18,566
And our whole Local Group of galaxies, Andromeda included, is moving at about
six hundred kilometres a second, drawn toward vast gatherings of galaxies:

31
00:03:18,566 --> 00:03:23,422
the Great Attractor, and beyond
it, the Shapley Supercluster.

32
00:03:27,922 --> 00:03:30,197
So why don't you feel any of it?

33
00:03:31,124 --> 00:03:33,106
Because you don't feel speed.

34
00:03:33,106 --> 00:03:38,390
You feel changes in speed. Speeding
up. Slowing down. Turning sharply.

35
00:03:39,317 --> 00:03:47,157
Almost all of this motion is smooth and steady. Galileo
noticed it below deck on a ship gliding over calm water:

36
00:03:47,157 --> 00:03:51,437
nothing tells you you're
moving. Newton made it a law.

37
00:03:52,364 --> 00:03:55,266
And everything around
you is moving with you.

38
00:03:55,266 --> 00:04:01,957
The chair. The air. The window. The whole planet.
Nothing rushes past, so nothing gives it away.

39
00:04:06,457 --> 00:04:11,452
Since this video began, you've travelled
nearly a hundred thousand kilometres.

40
00:04:11,452 --> 00:04:14,065
A quarter of the way to the Moon.

41
00:04:15,848 --> 00:04:17,703
You've never been still.

42
00:04:18,630 --> 00:04:20,500
Not for one second.

